    {
      "id": "msg-b2b461be99a9",
      "message_id": "<Bl_N4JWhFJs.2jWccpYABQAJ@blavaan.groups.google.com>",
      "from_name": "Jeffrey Girard",
      "from_email_hash": "3a83b59176488721e93b6e6d5f80a96fe3af9b77f1bc6d703e6eb84e67a34f9c",
      "date": "2020-04-30T13:44:47Z",
      "subject": "Warning about P_WAIC when using LOO",
      "subject_clean": "Warning about P_WAIC when using LOO",
      "in_reply_to": null,
      "references": [],
      "body_plain": "When calling blavFitIndices(), I am getting a warning about p_waic estimates and a recommendation to try loo. I'm confused because I had set pD = \"loo\". Am I misunderstanding something or is this warning a false positive?\n\nblavFitIndices(\n  fit1,\n  baseline.model = fit0,\n  pD = \"loo\",\n  rescale = \"devM\",\n  fit.measures = \"all\"\n)\n\n#> 7 (7.3%) p_waic estimates greater than 0.4. We recommend trying loo instead.\n#> 96 (100.0%) p_waic estimates greater than 0.4. We recommend trying loo instead.Some Pareto k diagnostic values are too high. See help('pareto-k-diagnostic') for details.\n#>\n#> 6 (6.2%) p_waic estimates greater than 0.4. We recommend trying loo instead.",
      "body_snippet": "When calling blavFitIndices(), I am getting a warning about p_waic estimates and a recommendation to try loo. I'm confused because I had set pD = \"loo\". Am I misunderstanding something or is this warn",
      "thread_id": "thread-b2b461be99a9",
      "thread_depth": 0,
      "month": "2020-04",
      "source_url": "https://groups.google.com/g/blavaan/c/Bl_N4JWhFJs/m/2jWccpYABQAJ"
    },
    {
      "id": "msg-f8d2650ac80c",
      "message_id": "<Bl_N4JWhFJs.1I97PCwFBQAJ@blavaan.groups.google.com>",
      "from_name": "Mauricio Garnier-Villarreal",
      "from_email_hash": "08ebbb3a3240f90758456c27abb5350aa3ee7847fb57fa6180507b3e5e6c8a7d",
      "date": "2020-04-30T15:08:48Z",
      "subject": "Re: Warning about P_WAIC when using LOO",
      "subject_clean": "Warning about P_WAIC when using LOO",
      "in_reply_to": "<Bl_N4JWhFJs.2jWccpYABQAJ@blavaan.groups.google.com>",
      "references": [
        "<Bl_N4JWhFJs.2jWccpYABQAJ@blavaan.groups.google.com>"
      ],
      "body_plain": "Jeffrey\n\nThat is correct, this is a false positive warning. It happens because we use the fitMeasures() function in the process. This estimates both the p_waic and p_loo. So, it shows those warnings\n\nA small recommendation, save this output in an object to be able to use the summary functions, etc\n\nfit_mcmc <- blavFitIndices(\n\n  fit1,\n  baseline.model = fit0,\n  pD = \"loo\",\n  rescale = \"devM\",\n  fit.measures = \"all\"\n\n)\n\nOn Thursday, April 30, 2020 at 8:44:47 AM UTC-5, Jeffrey Girard wrote:\n> When calling blavFitIndices(), I am getting a warning about p_waic estimates and a recommendation to try loo. I'm confused because I had set pD = \"loo\". Am I misunderstanding something or is this warning a false positive?\n>\n> blavFitIndices(\n>   fit1,\n>   baseline.model = fit0,\n>   pD = \"loo\",\n>   rescale = \"devM\",\n>   fit.measures = \"all\"\n> )\n>\n> #> 7 (7.3%) p_waic estimates greater than 0.4. We recommend trying loo instead.\n> #> 96 (100.0%) p_waic estimates greater than 0.4. We recommend trying loo instead.Some Pareto k diagnostic values are too high. See help('pareto-k-diagnostic') for details.\n> #>\n> #> 6 (6.2%) p_waic estimates greater than 0.4. We recommend trying loo instead.",
      "body_snippet": "Jeffrey\n\nThat is correct, this is a false positive warning. It happens because we use the fitMeasures() function in the process. This estimates both the p_waic and p_loo. So, it shows those warnings\n\n",
